            <div xml:id="d636">

                <pb facs="0920" n="804" sameAs="frus:frus1923v01/pg804"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.74/339</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Assistant Secretary of State</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Harrison</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of the British Embassy</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Craigie</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1923-06-28"><hi rendition="#italic">June 28,
                        1923</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">My Dear Craigie</hi>: Let me acknowledge your
                    personal letter of June 22nd,<anchor n="15" xml:id="d636-f1"/> asking whether I
                    would be in a position to give you, <pb facs="0921" n="805" xml:id="pg805"/>
                    unofficially and confidentially, the details in regard to the amendments to the
                    Federal Wireless contract, which that Company is seeking to have made by the
                    Chinese Government. I am very sorry to be compelled to reply that I cannot at
                    this time communicate the details of these amendments; but I am happy to be able
                    to assure you that they are not such as would substantially affect the contract
                    or be incompatible with any arrangements, on the basis of the recommendations of
                    the radio experts, dated February 4, 1922, which might eventually be arrived
                    at.</p>
                <p>Very sincerely young,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Leland Harrison</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
